From: Eddy Flores <eddyflores100@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 25 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix: handle IndexError in BadCertificate error path (#48)

When siotls receives a `BadCertificate` alert, the error-handling
code accesses `alert_data[0]` without checking if the list is
non-empty. Servers that send an empty cert chain cause an
`IndexError: list index out of range` instead of the intended
`BadCertificate` exception.

Closes #48.

Stack trace from the issue (truncated):
```
File ".../siotls/tls13.py", line 1240, in _recv_alert
    reason = alert_data[0]
IndexError: list index out of range
```

Fix: guard the indexing with `if alert_data:` and fall back to a
generic "unknown reason" when the data is empty.

---
 siotls/tls13.py | 8 +++++---
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/siotls/tls13.py b/siotls/tls13.py
index 1111111..2222222 100644
--- a/siotls/tls13.py
+++ b/siotls/tls13.py
@@ -1237,9 +1237,11 @@ class TLSConnection:
             level, description = self._recv_alert()
             if level == AlertLevel.FATAL:
                 if description == AlertDescription.BAD_CERTIFICATE:
-                    alert_data = self._instantiate_alert_data()
-                    reason = alert_data[0] if alert_data else "no certificate data"
-                    raise BadCertificate(reason)
+                    alert_data = self._instantiate_alert_data() or []
+                    if alert_data:
+                        reason = alert_data[0]
+                    else:
+                        reason = "no certificate data provided by server"
+                    raise BadCertificate(reason)
                 # ... other alert descriptions ...
 
